readonlyreturns_x86_64 -- the GH-1034 return-guard fixture for option `readonlyreturns`
(tests/stages/kuna-readonlyreturns.xml).

What it is
  A 28,352-byte x86-64 ELF built from readonlyreturns_x86_64.c, which is
  generated straight-line C. `exits` makes 256 calls to `sink`, reads one word
  of the read-only table `tab` (in .rodata, defined in inline assembly so the
  compiler cannot fold the reads) before each call, and returns early after
  each call when the result matches a constant, so the function has 257 `ret`
  instructions. The fall-through path stores the writable global `hits`.
  `joins` keeps `tab[0] ^ y` in a register across two calls to `sink` after
  its only read of the table.

Why it is vendored
  The op counts the stage test pins depend on the exact code layout, so the
  binary is checked in rather than built at test time (CI's gcc 15 would lay the
  function out differently).

Where it came from
  gcc 11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3), GNU ld 2.38, x86-64 Linux:

      gcc -O2 -fno-pie -no-pie -fno-inline -o readonlyreturns_x86_64 readonlyreturns_x86_64.c

  sha256 a6552d5491d8fe155d4ec6d0dd36f5b534bfbaf52fc330b4058b20fa74be4b0d.
  Run with no arguments it prints -2250365822891767781 -2250365822891767781
  -1362730841740883504.
